<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10829"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 07, 2005 - 5:47 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><i>&#34;can i get an amen from the Crazy people...&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>Preach it brother tell it! <BR> <BR>Honestly, I think there are certain things about this case that are actually much more interesting now that we know what BTK looks like, who he was pretending to be all those years. <BR> <BR>Because I do think the real man is BTK, and Dennis Rader was a front. Ever read William Diehl&#39;s &#34;Primal Fear&#34;, or see the movie starring Edward Norton and Richard Gere?  <BR> <BR>Norton played a young man who seemed afflicted with two personalities; the genteel, shy, retiring Aaron, and Roy, the vicious, highly intelligent killer. <BR> <BR>In the novel and movie, Gere&#39;s character, Aaron/Roy&#39;s attorney, brilliantly convinces, he thinks, the jury that Aaron/Roy is not guilty by reason of insanity.  <BR> <BR>But my favorite part, the one I think applies to any discussion of &#34;Dennis Rader&#34;, is the end, when  Martin Vail <i>&#40;the lawyer&#41;</i> is speaking with Aaron/Roy before he is to be taken to the mental hospital, and finally the boy says; <BR> <BR><i>&#34;...There never was an Aaron, counselor...&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>There never was, I believe, a &#34;Dennis&#34;. <BR> <BR>That&#39;s just one reason I&#39;m still around and posting about this.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10829-->
