<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10832"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:embersageblue@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 07, 2005 - 5:56 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hey  Steve funny you should bring that up That movie was on the other day and I completely agree. I can&#39;t remember right at the moment which Pyschologist came up with the Lookig Glass Theory, but is basically says that we act the way we believe others want us to act. we can not truly look into ourselves because we are more concerned with how to be acceptable to others, Serial Killers grow up apeing other people, since they can not form feelings of their own, they study other peopls actions and pretent to have those same feelings, everything in life is an act and they are better skilled at the art then the reat of us, because most of us don&#39;t have to analysis our emotions on a daily basis.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
