<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11108"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 09, 2005 - 9:22 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Embersage, it looks like you became a serial poster. Excessive Post Syndrome can be cured. Many who have suffered with this Ex Factor have learned to tame the monster that controls them. <BR>Good luck with that and we hope to see you soon. <BR>Recognizing EPS is the first step to recovery. <BR> <BR>I hope you know I&#39;m just kidding and don&#39;t stop posting here. Maybe you&#39;ve overposted, but in the long run so what! I&#39;d much rather read your posts than read nasty mean spirited ones directed against others that some others post around here. <BR> <BR>Anyway to answer Yomama&#39;s post, this was from the LA Times online that was posted on March 6th under the article titled &#39;Clues were Clear But Slow To Add Up&#39;. <BR>&#34;In January Rader became president of his congregation. When his printer at home brike, Rader used the church computer to print out an agenda for an upcoming meeting.&#34;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
